        Root cause:
        The root cause of the bug is the use of fixed-size stack records in the stack depot, which leads to inefficient memory usage when stack traces do not require eviction. This results in excessive memory allocation and wastage, particularly in scenarios where variable-sized stack traces are sufficient. The introduction of stack evictions necessitated a fixed size for records, but this approach is not optimal for non-evictable entries, causing significant memory overhead.

        Commit message:
        stackdepot: use variable size records for non-evictable
 entries

With the introduction of stack depot evictions, each stack record is now
fixed size, so that future reuse after an eviction can safely store
differently sized stack traces.  In all cases that do not make use of
evictions, this wastes lots of space.

Fix it by re-introducing variable size stack records (up to the max
allowed size) for entries that will never be evicted.  We know if an entry
will never be evicted if the flag STACK_DEPOT_FLAG_GET is not provided,
since a later stack_depot_put() attempt is undefined behavior.

